from pathlib import Path
import hashlib,re

root=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
expected={
'transported-radial-endpoint-realization':'a73b49fa4efab59afd76d2c435005b0902d56a762b232bcf02b6c50c2e289f0e',
'jost-volterra-solution':'7ee1a12ab386a7e3af1e69bdc517f34547754c403514d0205af64f25c9edde23',
'continuum-spectral-density':'516cad08648faf733e3926c55fccf0618ccbfb0804dce24faef17560882f79da',
'onto-radial-continuum-transform':'3209b65bee5949e4528b600fc89838f44477a95f3f9c193f04cb1e23756b8c27'}
texts={}
for key,h in expected.items():
 p=root/'items'/f'thm-nrqm-coulomb-{key}.md'
 assert hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest()==h,(key,'changed inputs')
 texts[key]=p.read_text()

def step(s,num,new):
 pattern=rf'(?m)^{re.escape(num)} .*?(?=\n\n\d+\.\d+ |\n\n## |\Z)'
 out,n=re.subn(pattern,lambda m:new.strip(),s,flags=re.S)
 assert n==1,(num,n)
 return out

k='transported-radial-endpoint-realization';s=texts[k]
s=s.replace('Let $\\gamma>0$, let','Let $\\gamma\\ge0$, let',1)
s=s.replace('self-adjoint operator of [[thm-nrqm-coulomb-infinitesimal-kinetic-bound]] (c),','self-adjoint operator of [[thm-nrqm-coulomb-infinitesimal-kinetic-bound]] (c) when $\\gamma>0$, and the free Fourier multiplier when $\\gamma=0$,')
s=step(s,'2.1',r'''2.1 [Boundedness of the form] For every $l$ and $u,v\in V_l$, step 1.1 gives $\|u/r\|_2\le2\|u'-u/r\|_2$. Cauchy-Schwarz therefore bounds the Coulomb pairing by $|\int u\overline v/r|\le\|u/r\|_2\|v\|_2\le2\|u'-u/r\|_2\|v\|_2$. The derivative pairing is bounded by the product of its derivative norms, and the angular pairing by $l(l+1)\|u/r\|_2\|v/r\|_2$. All three terms are consequently bounded by a constant times $\|u\|_{V_l}\|v\|_{V_l}$, including $l=0$ and $\gamma=0$. No replacement of the weight $r^{-1}$ by $1$ occurs. [step 1.1, F3, given, algebra]''')
s=s.replace('so $u\\in V_l$ exactly when $u\\in L^2$ and $u\'-u/r\\in L^2$ distributionally.','so $V_l$ is its graph-closure domain. On compactly supported smooth functions, expansion and integration of $(|u|^2/r)\' give $\\|u\'-u/r\\|_2^2=\\|u\'\\|_2^2$. Together with the Hardy estimate below this identifies $V_l$ with $H_0^1(0,\\infty)$, the closure of $C_c^\\infty(0,\\infty)$ in $H^1$, with equivalent norms. In particular a function with $u,u\',u/r\\in L^2$ and zero trace at $0$ belongs to $V_l$: cut off at $0$ using $\\int_0^{2\\varepsilon}|u|^2/r^2\\to0$, cut off at infinity, and smooth on compact subintervals.')
s=s.replace('$b_\\mu(u,u)\\ge\\tfrac12\\|u\\|^2_{V_l}$ by step 3.1','$b_\\mu(u,u)\\ge\\min\\{1/2,\\mu-M_l\\}\\|u\\|^2_{V_l}$ by step 3.1')
s=s.replace('$b_\\mu(u,u)\\ge\\tfrac12\\|u\\|^2_{V_l}\\ge0$','$b_\\mu(u,u)\\ge\\min\\{1/2,\\mu-M_l\\}\\|u\\|^2_{V_l}\\ge0$')
s=s.replace('we get $\\langle A_lu,u\\rangle\\ge-\\mu\\|u\\|_2^2$, so $A_l$ is bounded below.','the original form bound of step 3.1 gives $\\langle A_lu,u\\rangle=a_l(u,u)\\ge-M_l\\|u\\|_2^2$, so $A_l\\ge-M_l$.')
texts[k]=s

k='jost-volterra-solution';s=texts[k]
s=s.replace("f_l'=ik\\,e^{i(kr+a\\log r)}+O(1/r)","f_l'=e^{i(kr+a\\log r)}[ik+O(1/r)]")
s=s.replace('$\\int_R^L|g(r)|^2dr=(|A|^2+|B|^2)(L-R)+O(1)$','$\\int_R^L|g(r)|^2dr=(|A|^2+|B|^2)[L-R-\\gamma\\log(L/R)/(2k^2)]+O(1)$')
s=step(s,'1.1',r'''1.1 [The regular solution] On any compact set of $k$ choose $C\ge1$ with $C\ge\gamma$ and $C^2\ge|k|^2$. Induction on the recurrence gives $|c_n|\le C^n/n!$: the ratio of its bound to $C^n/n!$ is $[\gamma/C+|k|^2(n-1)/C^2]/(n+2l+1)\le n/(n+2l+1)\le1$, including $n=1$ with the absent second term. Thus the series and every fixed derivative converge uniformly on compact $(k,r)$-sets; polynomial coefficients and the uniform majorant give holomorphy in $k$. Direct coefficient substitution verifies the equation and $\varphi_l/r^{l+1}\to1$, uniformly on compact $k$-sets near $r=0$. [F1, F3, given, algebra]''')
s=step(s,'2.1',r'''2.1 [The Volterra kernel on a common tail] The exact modulus for $s\ge y$ is $|w(s)^2w(y)^{-2}|=\exp[-2\operatorname{Im}k(s-y)](s/y)^{-2\operatorname{Im}a}$, with $-2\operatorname{Im}a=\gamma\operatorname{Im}k/|k|^2$. Since $\log(s/y)\le(s-y)/y$, this modulus is at most one when $y\ge\gamma/(2|k|^2)$. On a compact subset of $K$ choose a common $r_0$ also satisfying $|a|/r_0\le|k|/2$. Then $|k+a/y|\ge|k|/2$ on the tail and the derivative of its inverse is $O(y^{-2})$, uniformly. Integration by parts using $(w^{-2})'=-2i(k+a/y)w^{-2}$ gives $|w(s)^2\int_r^s w(y)^{-2}dy|\le C$ and hence $|R(r,s)|\le C/s^2$. These estimates hold uniformly on compact $k$-sets, not by an incorrect sign in the power of $s/y$. [F3, given, algebra]''')
s=step(s,'4.1',r'''4.1 [Exact derivatives and parameter dependence] Retain the full double-integral fixed-point equation and put $I(r)=\int_r^\infty w(s)^2v(s)s^{-2}ds$. Its lower-limit boundary term is zero, so differentiating gives $v'=-b w(r)^{-2}I(r)$ and $v''=-2i(k+a/r)v'+bv/r^2$; the ratio estimate in step 2.1 gives $v'=O(r^{-1})$. With $f=wv$ the product rule yields $f''=[-k^2-\gamma/r+l(l+1)/r^2]f$, because $b=a^2+l(l+1)+ia$. Thus $f=w[1+O(r^{-1})]$ and $f'=w[ik+O(r^{-1})]$. On each compact parameter set, choose a common tail where the contraction constant is less than one; its uniformly convergent Picard iterates give continuity up to real $k$ by dominated convergence. On compact sets in the open upper half-plane the same kernel integrals are holomorphic by dominated contour integration; uniform Picard convergence gives holomorphy of $v$. Initial values at the common tail then extend these properties to each compact $r$-interval by [F1]. For nonreal $k\in K$, $f,f'$ decay exponentially times a fixed power, since $|w|=e^{-\operatorname{Im}k r}r^{-\operatorname{Im}a}$. [step 2.1, step 3.1, F1, F3, F4, algebra]''')
s=step(s,'6.1',r'''6.1 [The real-energy correction and absence of positive eigenvalues] For real $k>0$, write $J(r)=\int_r^\infty e^{2i\theta(s)}v(s)s^{-2}ds$, $h=v/r^2$ and $Q=2i\theta'$. The derivative identity gives initially $v'=O(r^{-1})$. Integration by parts gives $J=-e^{2i\theta(r)}h(r)/Q(r)-\int_r^\infty e^{2i\theta}(h/Q)'$, and $(h/Q)'=O(r^{-3})$, hence $J=O(r^{-2})$ and $v'=O(r^{-2})$. The ODE now gives $v''=O(r^{-2})$. A second integration by parts has $((h/Q)'/Q)'=O(r^{-4})$, so $J=-e^{2i\theta(r)}v(r)/(2i\theta'(r)r^2)+O(r^{-3})$. Therefore $v'=b/(2ik r^2)+O(r^{-3})$ and, integrating to $v(\infty)=1$, $v=1-b/(2ik r)+O(r^{-2})$. Since $-b/(2ik)=-a/(2k)+i(a^2+l(l+1))/(2k)$, $|f|^2=1-a/(kr)+O(r^{-2})$. Moreover $\int_R^L f^2$ is bounded uniformly in $L$: integration by parts in $e^{2i\theta}v^2$ uses $\theta'\ge k$, $\theta''=O(r^{-2})$, bounded $v$ and $v'=O(r^{-2})$, with an integrable differentiated amplitude. Expansion of $|Af+B\overline f|^2$ thus gives $(|A|^2+|B|^2)[L-R-(a/k)\log(L/R)]+O(1)$ after integration, where $a/k=\gamma/(2k^2)$. If it is square integrable then $A=B=0$, since the linear term dominates the logarithm. By [F6] and step 5.1 this excludes every positive eigenvalue of $A_l$. [step 4.1, step 5.1, F3, F6, algebra]''')
texts[k]=s

k='continuum-spectral-density';s=texts[k]
start=s.index('1.1 [The Green kernel');end=s.index('6.1 [No eigenvalue at zero]')
s=s[:start]+r'''1.1 [The actual endpoint-domain Green solution] Fix a nonreal $z=k^2$ with $k\in K$, $\operatorname{Im}k>0$, and compact support of $g$ in $[r_1,r_2]\subset(0,\infty)$. The regular solution has $\varphi_l=r^{l+1}[1+O(r)]$, $\varphi_l'=O(r^l)$ near zero, and the Jost solution and derivative decay exponentially times a power at infinity. If $W_l(k)=0$, the two solutions are proportional, and their common solution has $u,u',u/r\in L^2$, zero trace at zero, hence belongs to $V_l$; integration by parts extends the distributional equation to the form identity on $V_l$, putting it in $D(A_l)$ with eigenvalue $z$, impossible by self-adjointness. Thus $W_l(k)\ne0$. The corrected integral is $u(r)=-f_l(r)\int_0^r\varphi_l(s)g(s)/W_l\,ds-\varphi_l(r)\int_r^\infty f_l(s)g(s)/W_l\,ds$. Below $r_1$ it is a constant times $\varphi_l$, and above $r_2$ a constant times $f_l$. On the middle compact interval differentiation with $g\in L^2$ gives $u,u'$ locally absolutely continuous and $-u''+[l(l+1)/r^2-\gamma/r-z]u=g$. The endpoint bounds give $u,u',u/r\in L^2$ and zero trace, so $u\in V_l$. For compact test functions integration by parts gives $a_l(u,v)-z\langle u,v\rangle=\langle g,v\rangle$; boundedness of the form and density in $V_l$ extend it to every $v\in V_l$. Hence $u\in D(A_l)$ and $(A_l-z)u=g$. The inverse uniqueness gives the stated kernel with derivative jump $-1$ and its norm bound. [F1, F2, F6, given, algebra]

2.1 [Uniform compact-source boundary values] At real $k>0$, the Wronskian identity gives $\operatorname{Im}(\overline f W)=k\varphi_l$ and hence $\operatorname{Im}(-f/W)=k\varphi_l/|W|^2$. Therefore $\operatorname{Im}G(k^2+i0;r,s)=\varphi_l(k,r_<)\operatorname{Im}[-f_l(k,r_>)/W_l(k)]=k\varphi_l(k,r)\varphi_l(k,s)/|W_l(k)|^2$. On a compact positive energy interval, Jost parameter continuity on compact $r$-sets and the uniform regular series give uniform convergence of the kernel as $k=\sqrt{E+i\varepsilon}$ approaches the real interval. The real Wronskian is nonzero, so compactness gives a uniform positive lower bound for its modulus, also for sufficiently small $\varepsilon$. Since compact-source $g$ is in $L^1$ on its support, the resolvent pairing converges uniformly on this interval, with imaginary part $k|\int\varphi_l(k,r)g(r)dr|^2/|W_l(k)|^2$. [step 1.1, F2, F5, F6, algebra]

3.1 [Density without presupposing absent endpoint atoms] Put $\mu_g(B)=\langle E_{A_l}(B)g,g\rangle$. For a continuous real function $h$ with compact support in $(0,\infty)$, [F3] and Fubini express $\pi^{-1}\int h(E)\operatorname{Im}\langle(A_l-E-i\varepsilon)^{-1}g,g\rangle dE$ as $\int(P_\varepsilon*h)(\lambda)d\mu_g(\lambda)$, where $P_\varepsilon(t)=\varepsilon/[\pi(t^2+\varepsilon^2)]$. Uniform continuity of the zero extension of $h$ and the tail estimate $\int_{|t|>\delta}P_\varepsilon(t)dt\to0$ give uniform convergence $P_\varepsilon*h\to h$. For compact-source $g$, step 2.1 gives the other limit $\int h(E)\rho_l(E)|\int\varphi_l(\sqrt E,r)g(r)dr|^2dE$. Thus these continuous integrals agree. On each compact energy interval, distance cutoffs approximate indicators of closed sets, and finite regular measure uniqueness identifies $\mu_g$ with that continuous density on $(0,\infty)$. In particular all its positive atoms vanish and the interval identity holds without circularly assuming this fact in spectral inversion. [step 2.1, F3, F4, F6, algebra]

4.1 [General vectors and exact Poisson interval formula] For arbitrary $g$, choose compact-source $g_n\to g$. For every Borel $B$, projection boundedness gives $|\mu_g(B)-\mu_{g_n}(B)|\le(\|g\|+\|g_n\|)\|g-g_n\|$, uniformly in $B$. Every Lebesgue-null $B\subset(0,\infty)$ has $\mu_{g_n}(B)=0$ by step 3.1, so $\mu_g(B)=0$ by the uniform bound; in particular all positive atoms vanish. Integrating the resolvent Poisson kernel over $(a,b)$ now gives a bounded scalar function converging pointwise to $\mathbf1_{(a,b)}$ plus half the endpoint indicators. Dominated convergence with the now-zero endpoint atoms proves the exact interval formula for every $g$. [step 3.1, F3, F4, F6, algebra]

5.1 [The positive subspace is purely absolutely continuous] Step 4.1 says every scalar spectral measure restricted to $(0,\infty)$ is absolutely continuous. For $g$ in the positive spectral subspace, its entire measure is carried by $(0,\infty)$, so that subspace is absolutely continuous and contains no singular continuous or pure point part. This preserves the full positive-spectrum conclusion while making no assertion that compact-source vectors with negative spectral components are entirely absolutely continuous. [step 4.1, F3, algebra]

'''+s[end:]
texts[k]=s

k='onto-radial-continuum-transform';s=texts[k]
s=s.replace('(d) *complete Coulomb spectral resolution.* The operator','(d) *complete Coulomb spectral resolution.* If $\\gamma>0$, the operator')
s=s.replace('while the positive part is purely absolutely continuous.','while the positive part is purely absolutely continuous. If $\\gamma=0$, the representation is $\\bigoplus_{(l,m)}L^2((0,\\infty),dE)$ with multiplication by $E$ and no discrete copies, because the free Fourier multiplier has no nonpositive spectral projection.')
s=step(s,'2.1',r'''2.1 [Extension through the positive projection] Step 1.1 gives $\|T_lg\|\le\|g\|$ on compact sources, so extend $T_l$ first to a contraction $T$ on all $L^2$. Passing to compact-source approximations yields $\|Tg\|^2=\langle P_+g,g\rangle$ for every $g$, where $P_+=E_{A_l}((0,\infty))$. Thus $T(I-P_+)=0$ and $\widehat T=T|_{P_+H}$ is an isometry. Its range is closed because Cauchy sequences in that range pull back to Cauchy sequences in the complete positive subspace. In particular compact-source images are dense in its range, without incorrectly calling $T_l$ isometric on all compact sources. [step 1.1, F2, F3, algebra]''')
s=step(s,'3.1',r'''3.1 [The range actually reduces every indicator] Set $Q=\widehat T\widehat T^*$. The polarized density identity, first on intervals and then on all positive Borel sets by finite measure uniqueness, gives $T^*M_{\mathbf1_B}T=E_{A_l}(B)$. Hence $Q M_{\mathbf1_B}Tg=T E_{A_l}(B)g$. Moreover $\|M_{\mathbf1_B}Tg\|^2=\langle E_{A_l}(B)g,g\rangle=\|T E_{A_l}(B)g\|^2=\|Q M_{\mathbf1_B}Tg\|^2$. Pythagoras forces $(I-Q)M_{\mathbf1_B}Tg=0$. Compact-source images are dense in the range, so it is invariant under each self-adjoint indicator multiplier; its orthogonal complement is invariant as well. Thus $Q M_{\mathbf1_B}=M_{\mathbf1_B}Q$. [step 2.1, F1, F3, algebra]''')
s=step(s,'5.1',r'''5.1 [Exact bounded and unbounded diagonalization] Step 3.1 gives $\widehat T E_{A_l}(B)|_{P_+H}=M_{\mathbf1_B}\widehat T$ for every positive Borel set. Linear combinations and bounded pointwise simple approximation therefore give $\widehat T h(A_l)|_{P_+H}\widehat T^*=M_{h|_{(0,\infty)}}$ for every bounded Borel $h$. This uses the onto-ness of step 4.1 and does not incorrectly pair negative components through $T$. For unbounded energy, spectral truncation and the norm formula give $\int E^2|\widehat T g(E)|^2dE=\int_{(0,\infty)}\lambda^2dE_g(\lambda)$, so the exact generator domain is $\{h:Eh\in L^2(dE)\}$ and $\widehat T A_l|_{P_+H}\widehat T^*=M_E$ there. [step 3.1, step 4.1, F3, algebra]''')
s=s.replace('Assembling these unitaries gives the unitary $V$ and the diagonal form displayed in (d).','The absence of a zero eigenvector follows from [[thm-nrqm-coulomb-continuum-spectral-density]]. For $\\gamma>0$, assembling the normalized bound eigenvectors and positive transforms gives the stated unitary $V$. For $\\gamma=0$, use the transported free operator: its explicit Fourier multiplier $|p|^2$ has zero projection on $(-\\infty,0]$, since $\\{p=0\\}$ is null. Each free radial block is consequently wholly positive, and the same positive transforms assemble a continuum-only unitary. No zero-energy bound copies are introduced.')
texts[k]=s

for key,s in texts.items():
 (root/'items'/f'thm-nrqm-coulomb-{key}.md').write_text(s)
 print(key,hashlib.sha256(s.encode()).hexdigest())
